  "brief_description": "Christ is shown as a prisoner, a rope around his neck. He wears a crown of thorns and a purple robe, props made by his Roman captors to mock him: one of the charges against him was that he was claiming to be the 'King of the Jews'. Dressed like this, he was brought before the citizens of Jerusalem by Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor, with the words, 'Behold the Man!' (John 19: 5). In Latin this is Ecce Homo, which became the title of this type of image.\n\nMeditating upon the Passion (Christ's torture and execution) was thought to bring believers closer to God, and images like this were visual aids intended to promote empathy for Christ's physical suffering. The intensity of the image is achieved by the tight cropping around Christ's torso, and the dark background which contrasts with his strongly illuminated face -- visual devices usually reserved for portraits.",
